-- Migration: Question generation — REG batch 1 (30 questions)
-- Date: 2026-02-22
-- Topic: Estate and Gift Tax
-- Difficulty: 9E / 15M / 6H
-- Bloom's: L1:8 / L2:9 / L3:8 / L4:5

BEGIN;

INSERT INTO questions (section_id, topic, stem, choices, correct_index, explanation, difficulty, cognitive_level)
VALUES

-- Q1: medium / L2 — Per-donee annual exclusion computation
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'In 2026, Porter gives $25,000 to each of three unrelated friends and $15,000 to a nephew. Porter is unmarried and does not elect gift splitting. The annual exclusion is $19,000 per donee. What total amount must Porter report on Form 709 as taxable gifts?', '["$18,000", "$14,000", "$75,000", "$90,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 2503(b), the annual exclusion applies independently per donee. Porter''s three gifts of $25,000 each exceed the $19,000 exclusion by $6,000 per donee, totaling $18,000 in taxable gifts. The nephew''s $15,000 gift is below the exclusion and generates no taxable gift. Choice B is incorrect because it pools the nephew''s unused exclusion across other donees — the exclusion is per-donee and non-transferable.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q2: hard / L3 — QTIP trust inclusion at surviving spouse's death
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'Kessler died in 2020, and his estate elected QTIP treatment under IRC Section 2056(b)(7) for a $3,000,000 trust benefiting his surviving spouse, Rivera. Rivera received all trust income annually. Rivera dies in 2026 when the trust assets are worth $4,200,000. Analyze the estate tax consequence of the QTIP election for Rivera''s estate.', '["No amount is included in Rivera''s estate because Kessler''s estate already paid tax on the trust", "$4,200,000 is included in Rivera''s gross estate under Section 2044", "$3,000,000 is included based on the original trust value at Kessler''s death", "Only the $1,200,000 appreciation is included in Rivera''s gross estate"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 2044, the full value of QTIP trust assets at the surviving spouse''s death is included in the surviving spouse''s gross estate. Because Kessler''s estate received a marital deduction for the QTIP trust, Rivera''s estate must include the $4,200,000 current value. Choice A is incorrect because Section 2044 specifically requires inclusion as a trade-off for the prior marital deduction, rather than providing double taxation protection.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q3: medium / L3 — Three-year rule for life insurance transfers
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'On April 10, 2024, Matheson transferred ownership of a $750,000 life insurance policy on her own life to an irrevocable life insurance trust. Matheson dies on February 3, 2026. The policy death benefit is $750,000 and had a replacement cost of $28,000 at the time of transfer. Analyze the estate tax treatment of this policy.', '["Only the $28,000 replacement cost is included in Matheson''s gross estate", "No amount is included because the trust owned the policy at the date of death", "The full $750,000 death benefit is included in Matheson''s gross estate", "$722,000 is included, reflecting the death benefit minus replacement cost"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 2035(a), if a decedent transfers a life insurance policy within three years of death, the full death benefit is included in the gross estate under Section 2042. Matheson transferred the policy approximately 22 months before death, triggering the three-year rule. The $750,000 proceeds are fully includable. Choice B is incorrect because the three-year lookback overrides trust ownership for policies on the decedent''s life, unlike transfers of other property types.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q4: medium / L2 — Donee basis adjustment for gift tax paid
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'On June 1, 2026, Calloway gifts stock to his daughter, Drake. The stock has an adjusted basis of $120,000 and a fair market value of $320,000. Calloway pays $40,000 in federal gift tax on the transfer. What is Drake''s basis in the stock for purposes of determining gain?', '["$120,000", "$320,000", "$160,000", "$145,000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 1015(d)(6), a donee''s basis in gifted property equals the donor''s basis plus the portion of gift tax attributable to net appreciation. The appreciation is $200,000 ($320,000 FMV minus $120,000 basis). The adjustment equals $40,000 x ($200,000 / $320,000) = $25,000. Drake''s basis is $120,000 + $25,000 = $145,000. Choice C is incorrect because it adds the entire $40,000 gift tax rather than only the portion allocable to appreciation.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q5: hard / L4 — CRAT vs outright sale evaluation
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'Vaughn, age 70, owns appreciated stock worth $2,000,000 with a basis of $400,000. She wants to benefit the Lakeview Community Foundation while also generating retirement income. Her CPA recommends either (1) selling the stock, donating cash, and investing the remainder, or (2) transferring the stock to a charitable remainder annuity trust paying 6% annually. Evaluate which approach better achieves Vaughn''s combined income and charitable goals.', '["The CRAT is superior because it defers capital gains tax, provides a current income tax deduction, and generates a fixed annuity stream", "Selling the stock first is superior because it provides maximum flexibility and the charitable deduction offsets the capital gains", "The CRAT provides no income tax benefit because the charitable deduction is disallowed for split-interest gifts", "Both approaches produce identical tax results because the unified credit offsets any difference"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 664, a CRAT allows Vaughn to transfer appreciated property, avoid immediate recognition of the $1,600,000 capital gain, receive a 6% annuity ($120,000 annually), and claim a current charitable income tax deduction for the remainder interest. However, Choice B overlooks that selling first triggers immediate capital gains tax of approximately $336,000, significantly reducing the investable amount compared to the tax-deferred CRAT structure.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q6: easy / L2 — Below-market loan as imputed gift
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'On January 1, 2026, Saunders lends $200,000 to her adult son, Becker, interest-free for an indefinite term. The applicable federal rate is 5%. Under IRC Section 7872, what is the annual gift tax consequence?', '["No gift occurs because the transfer is a loan, not a gift", "A $200,000 gift is deemed to occur at the time of the loan", "A $19,000 gift is imputed, limited to the annual exclusion", "A $10,000 gift is imputed, equal to the forgone interest at the applicable federal rate"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 7872, a below-market loan is treated as a transfer of forgone interest from the lender to the borrower. The annual imputed gift equals the AFR applied to the outstanding principal: $200,000 x 5% = $10,000. Choice A is incorrect because Section 7872 specifically recharacterizes the interest differential as a gift, regardless of the transaction''s form as a loan.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q7: medium / L3 — General power of appointment and estate inclusion
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'Thornton holds a power to appoint the assets of a $1,500,000 trust to anyone she chooses, including herself or her creditors. Thornton did not create the trust and never exercised the power. She dies in 2026. Analyze whether the trust is included in Thornton''s gross estate.', '["The trust is excluded because Thornton never exercised the power during her lifetime", "The full $1,500,000 is included because Thornton held a general power of appointment at death", "Only the income interest is included because Thornton was a permissible beneficiary", "The trust is excluded because Thornton did not create the trust"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 2041(a)(2), property subject to a general power of appointment held at death is included in the gross estate regardless of whether the power was exercised. A power exercisable in favor of the holder, the holder''s estate, or creditors is a general power. The full $1,500,000 is includable. Choice A is incorrect because Section 2041 includes property merely because the power existed at death — exercise is not required.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q8: medium / L3 — Special use valuation eligibility under Section 2032A
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'Coleman dies in 2026 owning a 200-acre farm valued at $3,000,000 at highest and best use but only $1,800,000 as a working farm. The farm represents 60% of the adjusted gross estate. Coleman''s children plan to continue farming. The executor wants to elect special use valuation under Section 2032A. Analyze whether the election applies and its effect.', '["The election is unavailable because the farm value reduction exceeds a $500,000 statutory limit", "The election reduces the estate value by $3,000,000, removing the entire farm from the gross estate", "The election permits valuing the farm at $1,800,000, reducing the gross estate by $1,200,000", "The election is unavailable because the property must constitute at least 75% of the adjusted gross estate"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 2032A, qualifying real property used in farming or closely held business may be valued at current use rather than highest-and-best-use FMV. The farm exceeds the 50% adjusted-gross-estate threshold required for election. The $1,200,000 reduction falls within the statutory cap (approximately $1,390,000 for 2026). Choice D is incorrect because the actual thresholds are 50% for combined business property and 25% for the real property alone, rather than 75%.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q9: easy / L1 — Present interest requirement for annual exclusion
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'Under IRC Section 2503(b), what must a gift possess to qualify for the annual gift tax exclusion?', '["The gift must be made in cash or cash equivalents", "The gift must be made to a family member of the donor", "The gift must be irrevocable and complete", "The gift must be a present interest giving the donee immediate use or enjoyment"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 2503(b), the annual exclusion applies only to gifts of present interests, meaning the donee must have an unrestricted right to the immediate use, possession, or enjoyment of the property. Future interests do not qualify. While completeness is necessary for a transfer to be a taxable gift, Choice C is wrong because the specific annual exclusion requirement is the present-interest condition.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q10: easy / L1 — Section 2503(c) trust for minors
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'Under IRC Section 2503(c), what is the primary requirement for a trust for a minor to qualify gifts to the trust for the annual exclusion?', '["Trust property and income must be distributable to the beneficiary by age 21", "The trust must terminate upon the donor''s death", "The trustee must distribute all income annually to the minor", "The trust must have at least three named beneficiaries"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 2503(c), a gift to a trust for a minor qualifies for the annual exclusion if the property and income may be expended by or for the beneficiary before age 21 and will pass to the beneficiary at age 21. However, Choice C describes an income-distribution requirement that applies to certain other trust arrangements but is not the specific test under Section 2503(c).', 'easy', 1),

-- Q11: easy / L1 — Gift tax marital deduction for U.S. citizen spouses
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'What is the gift tax marital deduction for transfers between U.S. citizen spouses under IRC Section 2523?', '["A deduction limited to $19,000 per year per spouse", "An unlimited deduction for gifts to a U.S. citizen spouse", "A deduction capped at 50% of the gift''s fair market value", "A deduction available only for gifts of community property"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 2523(a), an unlimited marital deduction applies to gifts made to a spouse who is a U.S. citizen, meaning no gift tax is imposed regardless of the amount transferred. Choice A is incorrect because it conflates the marital deduction with the annual exclusion — the $19,000 limit applies per donee for non-spouse gifts, whereas spousal transfers receive an unlimited deduction.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q12: medium / L2 — Net gift computation
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'Pearson transfers property valued at $700,000 to an irrevocable trust for the benefit of his niece, Lancaster. The gift is a future interest, so no annual exclusion applies. Lancaster, as trustee, agrees to pay the resulting gift tax from the trust. The applicable marginal gift tax rate is 40%. Determine Pearson''s taxable gift under the net gift arrangement.', '["$700,000", "$420,000", "$500,000", "$280,000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 2512 and the net gift doctrine in Revenue Ruling 75-72, when the donee pays the gift tax, the taxable gift equals the property''s FMV divided by (1 + the tax rate). Here, $700,000 / 1.40 = $500,000, with the donee paying $200,000 in tax. Choice B is incorrect because it subtracts 40% directly from the FMV rather than solving the circular computation where the tax reduces the taxable gift amount.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q13: medium / L3 — Charitable lead trust vs charitable remainder trust comparison
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'Hoffman, age 65, owns appreciated stock worth $5,000,000 with a basis of $800,000. He wants to transfer wealth to his children while supporting the Clearwater Arts Foundation. His advisor compares a charitable lead annuity trust (CLAT) to a charitable remainder unitrust (CRUT). Hoffman''s primary goal is reducing the taxable gift to his children. Which strategy best achieves this goal?', '["The CRUT, because the charitable remainder eliminates the gift entirely", "The CRUT, because income tax deferral on the stock sale reduces the overall transfer tax", "The CLAT, because the IRS disallows split-interest deductions for unitrusts", "The CLAT, because the present value of the annuity payments to charity reduces the taxable gift of the remainder interest"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 2522, a CLAT provides annuity payments to charity for a term, with the remainder passing to non-charitable beneficiaries. The taxable gift equals the total transfer minus the present value of the charitable annuity stream, directly reducing the gift to Hoffman''s children. Choice A is incorrect because a CRUT''s remainder goes to charity rather than to the children — it achieves the opposite of Hoffman''s primary wealth-transfer goal.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q14: medium / L3 — GST exemption allocation and taxable termination
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'Estrada created a trust in 2015 providing income to her daughter, Walsh, for life, with the remainder to Walsh''s children. Walsh dies in 2026 when the trust is worth $2,500,000. Estrada''s GST exemption was fully allocated to the trust at creation. Analyze the generation-skipping transfer tax consequence when Walsh dies.', '["No GST tax is due because the trust''s inclusion ratio is zero from the exemption allocation", "A taxable termination occurs and GST tax of approximately $1,000,000 is due at the 40% rate", "A direct skip occurs when the grandchildren receive the remainder", "The trust assets are included in Walsh''s estate rather than being subject to GST tax"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 2612(a), Walsh''s death triggers a taxable termination because the interest of a non-skip person (Walsh) terminates and remaining trust property passes to skip persons (grandchildren). However, because Estrada fully allocated her GST exemption at trust creation, the inclusion ratio is zero under Section 2642, resulting in no GST tax. Choice B correctly identifies the event type but is incorrect because it ignores the exemption allocation that zeroes out the tax.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q15: hard / L4 — Section 6166 installment payment evaluation
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'Navarro dies in 2026 with a gross estate of $22,000,000. The estate includes a 45% interest in Navarro Landscaping LLC, valued at $9,900,000. The remaining estate consists of publicly traded securities and real estate. The executor is evaluating whether to elect installment payment of estate tax under IRC Section 6166. Evaluate whether the estate qualifies and recommend the appropriate course of action.', '["The estate qualifies because the closely held business exceeds 35% of the adjusted gross estate, allowing the executor to defer tax attributable to the business interest over 14 years", "The estate does not qualify because Navarro owned less than 50% of the LLC", "The estate qualifies, but the deferral applies to the entire $3,000,000 estate tax liability", "The estate does not qualify because Section 6166 is limited to sole proprietorships"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 6166(a), estate tax attributable to a closely held business interest may be paid in installments over up to 14 years if the business exceeds 35% of the adjusted gross estate. Navarro''s 45% LLC interest ($9,900,000) represents 45% of the $22,000,000 gross estate, exceeding the 35% threshold. Choice C is incorrect because the deferral applies only to tax attributable to the business interest, rather than the full estate tax liability.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q16: easy / L1 — State death tax deduction under Section 2058
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'Under IRC Section 2058, what is the estate tax treatment of state death taxes paid by an estate?', '["State death taxes are credited dollar-for-dollar against the federal estate tax", "State death taxes paid are allowed as a deduction from the gross estate", "State death taxes are not deductible or creditable for federal purposes", "State death taxes reduce the applicable exclusion amount"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 2058, state death taxes paid are allowed as a deduction from the gross estate in computing the taxable estate. The former state death tax credit under Section 2011 was repealed for decedents dying after 2004 and replaced with this deduction. Choice A is incorrect because the pre-2005 credit no longer applies, whereas the current law provides only a deduction.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q17: medium / L2 — Community property estate inclusion computation
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'Martinez and Reyes are married and reside in a community property state. Martinez dies in 2026. Their community property includes a home worth $800,000, investments worth $600,000, and a joint bank account with $200,000. Martinez also holds separate property worth $500,000 inherited from his parents. Determine the amount included in Martinez''s gross estate.', '["$2,100,000", "$1,600,000", "$1,300,000", "$800,000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 2033 and community property law, only the decedent''s one-half share of community property is included in the gross estate, plus all separate property. Martinez''s estate includes $800,000 (half of $1,600,000 community property) plus $500,000 separate property, totaling $1,300,000. Choice B is incorrect because it includes the full community property, whereas Reyes owns the other half by operation of community property law.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q18: easy / L1 — QDOT requirement for non-citizen surviving spouse
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'What is the purpose of a qualified domestic trust (QDOT) under IRC Section 2056A?', '["To provide a stepped-up basis for property transferred to a surviving spouse", "To allow portability of the deceased spouse''s unused exemption", "To exempt property from generation-skipping transfer tax", "To allow the marital deduction when the surviving spouse is not a U.S. citizen"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 2056A, a QDOT is required to claim the estate tax marital deduction when the surviving spouse is not a U.S. citizen. The QDOT ensures estate tax will be collected when trust principal is distributed to the non-citizen spouse or at the spouse''s death. Choice B is incorrect because portability under Section 2010(c) is a separate election unrelated to the QDOT requirement.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q19: hard / L4 — Family limited partnership valuation discount assessment
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'Wellington, age 75, holds investment real estate worth $12,000,000. Her estate planner proposes transferring the real estate to a family limited partnership, then gifting 35% limited partnership interests to each of her two children over five years. The planner projects a 30% combined minority and marketability discount on the gifted interests. Assess the viability of this strategy under current estate tax law.', '["The strategy is invalid because IRC Section 2704 eliminates all valuation discounts for family entities", "The strategy will withstand IRS scrutiny because discounts are always permitted for limited partnership interests", "The strategy may be challenged if the IRS determines the partnership lacks a legitimate business purpose beyond tax reduction", "The strategy is unnecessary because the $12,000,000 is below the estate tax exemption threshold"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 2703 and case law including Estate of Bongard, the IRS may challenge FLP discounts when the partnership was formed primarily for tax avoidance without a legitimate nontax business purpose. Courts examine factors including commingling of personal assets, timing of transfers, and whether the entity operates as a genuine business. However, Choice A overstates Section 2704, which restricts certain liquidation restrictions but does not categorically eliminate valuation discounts.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q20: medium / L3 — Section 2036 retained income interest analysis
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'On March 1, 2022, Patterson transferred her rental property (then worth $900,000) to an irrevocable trust for her grandchildren but retained the right to receive all rental income for ten years. Patterson dies on July 15, 2026, when the property is worth $1,100,000. Analyze the estate tax treatment of the rental property.', '["The full $1,100,000 date-of-death value is included in Patterson''s gross estate under Section 2036", "Only the present value of the remaining income stream is included in Patterson''s gross estate", "The $900,000 transfer-date value is included because transfers are frozen at the date of gift", "No amount is included because the transfer was irrevocable and complete for gift tax purposes"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 2036(a)(1), if a decedent transfers property but retains the right to income for life or a period not ascertainable without reference to death, the full date-of-death value is included in the gross estate. Patterson retained rental income, triggering Section 2036, so the $1,100,000 current value is includable. Although the transfer was irrevocable, Choice D is incorrect because Section 2036 overrides irrevocability when the transferor retains income rights.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q21: easy / L1 — Non-spousal joint tenancy inclusion rule
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'Under IRC Section 2040(a), how is jointly held property between non-spouses included in a decedent''s gross estate?', '["Fifty percent of the property value is automatically included regardless of contributions", "The full value is included except to the extent the survivor can prove original contribution", "No portion is included because joint tenancy passes outside the probate estate", "The property is included at the decedent''s fractional share based on the number of co-owners"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 2040(a), for non-spousal joint tenancy, the full value of jointly held property is included in the first decedent''s gross estate unless the surviving joint tenant can demonstrate their proportionate contribution to the acquisition. This is the consideration-furnished test. Unlike the spousal rule under Section 2040(b) which automatically includes 50%, the non-spousal rule presumes full inclusion.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q22: medium / L2 — Part-gift/part-sale basis computation
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'On October 5, 2026, Reynolds sells land with a fair market value of $350,000 and an adjusted basis of $100,000 to her daughter, Dawson, for $150,000. The transaction is treated as a part-gift/part-sale. Determine Dawson''s basis in the land for purposes of calculating gain on a later sale.', '["$100,000", "$350,000", "$150,000", "$250,000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under Treasury Regulation Section 1.1015-4, in a part-gift/part-sale transaction, the donee''s basis for determining gain is the greater of the amount paid or the transferor''s adjusted basis. Since Dawson paid $150,000, which exceeds Reynolds'' $100,000 basis, Dawson''s basis for gain is $150,000. Choice D is incorrect because computing basis as the difference between FMV and amount paid has no support in the regulations, unlike the greater-of test that actually applies.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q23: medium / L2 — GST inclusion ratio computation
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'Fleming creates an irrevocable trust in 2026 with $4,000,000, naming her grandchildren as beneficiaries. Fleming allocates $3,000,000 of her remaining GST exemption to the trust on a timely filed gift tax return. Determine the trust''s inclusion ratio for generation-skipping transfer tax purposes.', '["0.00", "0.25", "0.75", "1.00"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 2642(a), the inclusion ratio equals 1 minus the applicable fraction (GST exemption allocated divided by the value of property transferred). Here, the applicable fraction is $3,000,000 / $4,000,000 = 0.75, yielding an inclusion ratio of 0.25. Future GST taxable events will be taxed at 25% of the maximum GST rate. However, Choice C confuses the applicable fraction (0.75) with the inclusion ratio — these are complementary values.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q24: medium / L2 — Cumulative gift tax computation
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'In 2024, Boyd made taxable gifts of $1,000,000 and paid no gift tax due to the unified credit. In 2026, Boyd makes additional taxable gifts of $500,000. The gift tax rate on the combined $1,500,000 in cumulative taxable gifts produces a tentative tax of $555,800, and the tentative tax on the $1,000,000 prior gifts is $345,800. Determine Boyd''s 2026 gift tax before applying the unified credit.', '["$555,800", "$345,800", "$500,000", "$210,000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 2502(a), gift tax is computed cumulatively. The current year''s tax equals the tentative tax on all cumulative taxable gifts minus the tentative tax on prior-period taxable gifts: $555,800 minus $345,800 = $210,000. This amount is then offset by any remaining unified credit. Choice A is incorrect because it uses the full tentative tax on cumulative gifts rather than subtracting the tax attributable to prior gifts already accounted for.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q25: easy / L1 — General vs special power of appointment
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'Under IRC Section 2041, what distinguishes a general power of appointment from a special (limited) power of appointment?', '["A general power allows the holder to appoint property to themselves, their estate, or their creditors", "A general power requires court approval before assets can be distributed", "A special power allows the holder to appoint property to anyone without restriction", "A general power applies only to property held in revocable trusts"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 2041(b)(1), a general power of appointment is one exercisable in favor of the holder, the holder''s estate, the holder''s creditors, or creditors of the holder''s estate. A special (limited) power restricts appointees to a defined class excluding these parties. Choice C reverses the definitions — unrestricted appointment authority characterizes a general power, while a special power is specifically limited.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q26: medium / L3 — Section 2038 retained power to alter beneficial enjoyment
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'Garrett created a trust in 2018 and retained the power to alter, amend, or revoke the trust and change the beneficiaries. In 2024, Garrett amended the trust to remove the revocation power but retained the power to change beneficiaries. Garrett dies in 2026 when the trust holds $2,800,000. Analyze the estate tax treatment of the trust assets.', '["No amount is included because Garrett removed the revocation power before death", "The full $2,800,000 is included because Garrett retained the power to alter beneficial enjoyment", "Only the value at the time the revocation power was removed in 2024 is included", "The trust is included only if Garrett actually exercised the power to change beneficiaries"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 2038(a)(1), property is included in the gross estate if the decedent retained the power to alter, amend, revoke, or terminate the transfer. Although Garrett removed the revocation power, retaining the power to change beneficiaries constitutes a power to alter beneficial enjoyment, triggering full inclusion. Choice A is incorrect because Section 2038 encompasses any retained power to change the enjoyment of transferred property, not just revocation.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q27: medium / L2 — Incomplete gift doctrine
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'On May 1, 2026, Patel transfers $800,000 to a trust but retains the power to redirect distributions among the trust beneficiaries. Patel does not retain any beneficial interest in the trust. For gift tax purposes, determine whether the transfer is a completed gift.', '["The transfer is a completed gift of $800,000 because legal title passed to the trust", "The transfer is a completed gift of $781,000 after applying the annual exclusion", "The transfer is partially complete — only the income interest is a completed gift", "The transfer is an incomplete gift because Patel retained dominion and control over beneficial enjoyment"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under Treasury Regulation Section 25.2511-2(c), a gift is incomplete if the donor retains the power to change the beneficial interests in the transferred property. Patel''s retained power to redirect distributions among beneficiaries means the gift is not complete for gift tax purposes. Choice A is incorrect because transferring legal title alone does not complete the gift — the controlling test is whether the donor relinquished dominion and control, rather than mere ownership.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q28: hard / L4 — QPRT strategy evaluation
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'Chambers, age 68, owns a primary residence valued at $3,500,000. Her estate planner recommends a qualified personal residence trust with a 12-year retained interest. If Chambers survives the trust term, the home passes to her children at a significantly reduced gift tax value. If she dies during the term, the full date-of-death value is included under Section 2036. Evaluate whether this strategy is appropriate given Chambers''s circumstances.', '["The QPRT is inappropriate because Section 2036 will cause automatic estate inclusion of the residence", "The QPRT is appropriate regardless of mortality risk because the retained interest always reduces gift tax", "The QPRT is appropriate if Chambers''s life expectancy reasonably exceeds the 12-year term, balancing transfer tax savings against the Section 2036 inclusion risk", "The QPRT is inappropriate because only vacation homes qualify for QPRT treatment"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 2702(a)(3)(A)(ii), a QPRT transfers a personal residence at a reduced gift value based on the present value of the retained term interest. The critical risk is the grantor''s survival — if Chambers dies within 12 years, Section 2036 pulls the full value back into her estate, negating the benefit. At age 68, a 12-year term means surviving to age 80, making life expectancy analysis essential. Choice A overstates the risk by treating inclusion as certain rather than contingent on mortality.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q29: hard / L4 — Credit shelter trust vs portability evaluation
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'Baker dies in 2026 with a $20,000,000 estate, leaving everything to his wife, Chen. Their combined estates total $35,000,000. Chen''s estate planner evaluates two strategies: (1) relying on portability to preserve Baker''s unused exclusion, or (2) funding a credit shelter trust with Baker''s exclusion amount and passing the remainder to Chen. Chen is concerned about potential legislative reduction of the exclusion. Evaluate which strategy better protects against this risk.', '["The credit shelter trust is superior because it permanently removes assets from both estates and protects against future exclusion reductions", "Portability is superior because the DSUE amount is locked in at Baker''s death and cannot be reduced by legislation", "Both strategies produce identical results regardless of future law changes", "Portability is superior because the credit shelter trust triggers immediate estate tax on Baker''s death"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 2010(c), portability preserves the deceased spouse''s unused exclusion (DSUE), but the DSUE amount could be subject to future legislative changes or lost if Chen remarries. A credit shelter trust permanently removes Baker''s exclusion amount from both estates and shields appreciation from future estate tax. Choice B is incorrect because Congress can modify portability rules prospectively, and the DSUE is not permanently protected against legislative reduction.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q30: easy / L1 — Skip person definition for GST
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'Under IRC Section 2613, who is classified as a skip person for generation-skipping transfer tax purposes?', '["Any beneficiary who is younger than the transferor", "A natural person assigned to a generation two or more generations below the transferor", "The transferor''s child regardless of age", "Any person who is not a lineal descendant of the transferor"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 2613(a), a skip person is an individual assigned to a generation two or more below the transferor under Section 2651. Typically, grandchildren and more remote descendants qualify. Choice A is incorrect because generation assignment is based on family relationship or age differential for non-family members, rather than simply being younger than the transferor.', 'easy', 1)
;

COMMIT;
